203	\begin{lemma}[Albanese property at every point, in every genus]
204	  \label{lem:pic0_isAlbanese_all_points}
205	  \lean{AlgebraicGeometry.isAlbanese_pic0}
206	  \uses{def:IsAlbanese, thm:albanese_universal_property_northstar}
207	  Let \(C/k\) be a smooth proper geometrically integral curve whose Picard functor
208	  is representable. Then for every \(P\in C(k)\), the scheme \(\Pic^0_{C/k}\)
209	  satisfies the Albanese universal property for the pointed curve \((C,P)\).
210	\end{lemma}
211	
212	\begin{proof}
213	  For \(g(C) > 0\) and \(k\) algebraically closed this is
214	  \cref{thm:albanese_universal_property_northstar}, via the Abel--Jacobi morphism
215	  \(\iota_P\).
216	
217	  Two extensions are required. Over a general \(k\), apply the algebraically
218	  closed case over \(k^s\) and descend: the universal morphism and the factoring
219	  homomorphism are unique, so they are Galois-equivariant and descend to \(k\).
220	  For \(g(C) = 0\) the tangent space \(H^1(C,\mathcal O_C)\) vanishes, so
221	  \(\Pic^0_{C/k}\) is a smooth proper group scheme of dimension \(0\), namely
222	  \(\Spec k\); the universal property then asserts that every pointed morphism
223	  from \(C\) to an abelian variety is constant, which is the rigidity statement
224	  that a morphism from a genus-\(0\) curve to an abelian variety is constant.
225	\end{proof}
